Standard form then follows by the slack/surplus and objective-sign rules above. This is exactly the linearization from Part 2, made legitimate by the minimization direction. PART 5 — JULIA CRASH COURSE The last part of recitation implements and solves a basic optimization model in Julia using the JuMP modeling language (with an LP/MIP solver such as HiGHS or Gurobi). The pattern is: create a model, declare variables with their bounds, add the objective, add the constraints, optimize, and read back the solution. In outline: using JuMP, HiGHS
    model = Model(HiGHS.Optimizer)
    @variable(model, x_A >= 0)
    @variable(model, x_B >= 0)
    @objective(model, Max, 5x_A + 4x_B)
    @constraint(model, labor, 2x_A + x_B <= 100)
    @constraint(model, material, x_A + 2x_B <= 80)
    @constraint(model, contract, x_B >= 10)
    optimize!(model)
    value(x_A), value(x_B), objective_value(model) The mapping from the optimization skeleton to JuMP is one-to-one: @variable declares decision variables (with sign/bound restrictions), @objective states f and its sense (Min or Max), @constraint encodes each g_i, optimize! solves, and value(...) / objective_value(...) return x* and f(x*). This is the workflow you will reuse on the homework's computational parts.
